\section{Illustrative Walkthrough}
\label{sec:walkthrough}

\F~\ref{fig:walkthrough}(a) shows a program that extends the crop of
\F~\ref{fig:intro-crop}, and we now follow it through the compiler. The
columns of the image $a$ are stored sorted by \texttt{key}, and the program
restores their original order before it crops the image to the bounding box of
its nonzero pixels. Lines~2--3 compute the rank $c_j$ of each column, its
position in the sorted order, and line~4 reads the columns back in their
original order. Lines~5--8 are the crop, which here indexes the first and last
nonzero row and column directly. NumPy therefore rejects an image without
nonzero pixels, since \texttt{rows[0]} is then out of range.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \input{fig/tikz/walkthrough}
  \caption{\textbf{Compiling the running example.} (a)~The program. (b)~Arrays and
  facts on one input. (c)~The graph, with the selected subgraph shaded.}
  \label{fig:walkthrough}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Symbolic execution.}
Since $p$ is the result of \texttt{np.argsort}, it is a permutation \wfact{1},
and the assignment on line~3 becomes one scatter operator. Every $c_j$ is one of
the values of \texttt{np.arange(6)} \wfact{2}, so the reads on line~4 need no
index check. The flags $h$ and $g$ mark the nonzero rows and columns of
$\mathit{im}$. The cells of \texttt{rows} are the positions of the set flags of
$h$ in increasing order, and its runtime length is $\ell_r = \sum_i h_i$.
\texttt{rows[0]} and \texttt{rows[-1]} are therefore the first and last set
flag, $r_0$ and $r_1$. They become scan operators, and \texttt{rows} itself is
never built. Their index check, $\ell_r \ge 1$, remains an assertion, and so
does $\ell_c \ge 1$ for the columns. Both scans lie in $[0, 4]$, and $r_0 \le
r_1$ because they scan the same flags \wfact{3}. The slice therefore needs
neither Python's wrap nor its clamp, and its height $r_1 + 1 - r_0$ needs no
$\max(0, \cdot)$.

The slice becomes a window operator, whose output is the only array of the
program with a shape that depends on the input. Since a slice with runtime
bounds keeps the bounds of the sliced array, the output has the bounded type
$\langle \texttt{Int};\, 5, 6 \mid 30 \rangle$ \wfact{4}. The circuit thus reserves
the $5 \times 6$ cells of $\mathit{im}$ for it, the cropped image fills the
first $r_1 + 1 - r_0$ rows and $c_1 + 1 - c_0$ columns of these cells, and the
remaining cells hold padding.

\paragraph{Selection.}
The selected construction nodes form the two shaded subgraphs of
\F~\ref{fig:walkthrough}(c). The sort takes the order of the keys from the
prover and checks that the pairs of key and index increase along it, which
establishes that $p$ is a permutation. The scatter-add relies on this fact. It
computes the tests $[p_k = j]$ for every $k$ and $j$, and it provides them as
the one-hot vector of each $c_j$, which is column $j$ of the matrix in the
figure. Each read is then a sum $\mathit{im}_{ij} = \sum_k [p_k = j]\, a_{ik}$,
and $c$ itself is never computed. The scans use running products and check that
their one-hot vector sums to one, which holds exactly when some flag is set. They
thereby establish $\ell_r \ge 1$ and $\ell_c \ge 1$, the claims of the index
checks, so these assertions are not selected. The one-hot vectors $E$ of the
scans also let the window shift the rows and columns of $\mathit{im}$ without
comparing $r_0$ and $c_0$ with every position.

Selected one operator at a time, each scan would use a select chain, which is
cheaper on its own but provides neither the one-hot vectors nor the claims of
the index checks. The window would then compare its bounds with every position,
and the assertions would remain. The circuit has \ph{X} cells, against \ph{Y}
when each operator is selected on its own. The sort leaves the prover no choice,
since the order that it checks is unique. By \Thm~\ref{thm:lowering}, the
correctness of the selected constructions then composes into the correctness of
the circuit.
